From: Elie Bursztein <github@elie.net>
Subject: [PATCH] virtiofs: allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP

FUSE marks every superblock SB_I_NOIDMAP and clears it only when the
daemon negotiates FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP on a default_permissions mount. Once
cleared, fuse_get_req stops sending the caller's fsuid and fsgid: requests
that are not inode creation carry FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID, which only an
idmap-aware daemon accepts.

Apple's Virtualization.framework daemon never negotiates FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP
and reports every entry as owned by the ids in the request header.
mount_setattr(MOUNT_ATTR_IDMAP) on the share failed with EINVAL, so a
workload in a user namespace could not own its workspace, and chown,
tar -x and cp -a failed as container root.

Separate the decisions:

- virtio-fs, which always mounts with default_permissions so the guest
  kernel makes every permission decision through the mount's idmap,
  clears SB_I_NOIDMAP itself;
- request headers follow the idmap only when the daemon negotiated
  FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP (fc->idmap_headers). Sending FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID to
  Apple's daemon made it refuse ordinary requests, and the VM could not
  boot;
- otherwise virtio-fs sends the caller's ids as its own user namespace
  sees them (fc->caller_ns_ids). Apple's daemon reflects them back as the
  owner, so container root's entries are on-disk root's, which the
  idmapped workspace shows as container root's. Sending the VM-wide ids
  instead made them VM uid 100000, outside the map, and the workload could
  not enter a directory it had just made. Callers in the initial user
  namespace send exactly what they sent before.

Nothing changes for a mount nobody idmaps, or for a daemon that ignores
header ids (Capsem's own KVM server).

---
--- a/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
+++ b/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
@@ -898,6 +898,12 @@
 
 	/** Passthrough support for read/write IO */
 	unsigned int passthrough:1;
+
+	/** Server negotiated FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP: request headers follow the idmap */
+	unsigned int idmap_headers:1;
+
+	/** Headers carry the caller's ids as its own user namespace sees them */
+	unsigned int caller_ns_ids:1;
 
 	/* Use pages instead of pointer for kernel I/O */
 	unsigned int use_pages_for_kvec_io:1;
--- a/fs/fuse/inode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/inode.c
@@ -1443,10 +1443,12 @@
 			if (flags & FUSE_NO_EXPORT_SUPPORT)
 				fm->sb->s_export_op = &fuse_export_fid_operations;
 			if (flags & FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP) {
-				if (fc->default_permissions)
+				if (fc->default_permissions) {
 					fm->sb->s_iflags &= ~SB_I_NOIDMAP;
-				else
+					fc->idmap_headers = 1;
+				} else {
 					ok = false;
+				}
 			}
 			if (flags & FUSE_OVER_IO_URING && fuse_uring_enabled())
 				fc->io_uring = 1;
--- a/fs/fuse/dev.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/dev.c
@@ -198,7 +198,22 @@
 {
 	struct fuse_conn *fc = fm->fc;
 	struct fuse_req *req;
-	bool no_idmap = !fm->sb || (fm->sb->s_iflags & SB_I_NOIDMAP);
+	/*
+	 * A server that did not negotiate FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP gets the caller's
+	 * fsuid and fsgid, as before idmapped mounts, even on a superblock
+	 * that allows them (virtio-fs): only an idmap-aware server can take
+	 * FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID on requests that are not inode creation.
+	 */
+	bool no_idmap = !fm->sb || (fm->sb->s_iflags & SB_I_NOIDMAP) ||
+			!fc->idmap_headers;
+	/*
+	 * Apple's virtio-fs server reports every entry as owned by the ids
+	 * in the request header. Sent as the caller's own namespace sees
+	 * them, container root's entries come back as on-disk root's, which
+	 * the workspace's idmapped mount shows as container root's.
+	 */
+	struct user_namespace *ids_ns = no_idmap && fc->caller_ns_ids ?
+					current_user_ns() : fc->user_ns;
 	kuid_t fsuid;
 	kgid_t fsgid;
 	int err;
@@ -248,8 +263,8 @@
 	 */
 	fsuid = no_idmap ? current_fsuid() : mapped_fsuid(idmap, fc->user_ns);
 	fsgid = no_idmap ? current_fsgid() : mapped_fsgid(idmap, fc->user_ns);
-	req->in.h.uid = from_kuid(fc->user_ns, fsuid);
-	req->in.h.gid = from_kgid(fc->user_ns, fsgid);
+	req->in.h.uid = from_kuid(ids_ns, fsuid);
+	req->in.h.gid = from_kgid(ids_ns, fsgid);
 
 	if (no_idmap && unlikely(req->in.h.uid == ((uid_t)-1) ||
 				 req->in.h.gid == ((gid_t)-1))) {
--- a/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
@@ -1583,6 +1583,22 @@
 	err = fuse_fill_super_common(sb, ctx);
 	if (err < 0)
 		goto err_free_fuse_devs;
+
+	/*
+	 * Allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP from the daemon.
+	 * virtio-fs always mounts with default_permissions, so the guest
+	 * kernel makes every permission decision through the mount's idmap.
+	 * Apple's Virtualization.framework daemon never negotiates the flag
+	 * and reports every entry as owned by the requester, so the request
+	 * header carries the caller's ids as its own namespace sees them
+	 * (fuse_get_req): a user-namespaced workload then owns what it
+	 * touches through the idmapped workspace. Only a mount explicitly
+	 * idmapped with mount_setattr changes.
+	 */
+	if (fc->default_permissions) {
+		sb->s_iflags &= ~SB_I_NOIDMAP;
+		fc->caller_ns_ids = 1;
+	}
 
 	for (i = 0; i < fs->nvqs; i++) {
 		struct virtio_fs_vq *fsvq = &fs->vqs[i];
